 by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -7 \\\\ -5 & 2 & -9 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 22 & -44 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{22}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 22 & -44 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065011/state.json" "file_sha256" "f71acf576412692875148eeaadb4b7b5e216ba24093d8c4069b439789e36eb9f"} {"question" "q-35292" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065011/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ad3f809a708b8c734e70cd58d6c8fe9d69e72465e11bf4865226f3aec4350e51"} {"question" "q-35292" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065011/state.json" "file_sha256" "f71acf576412692875148eeaadb4b7b5e216ba24093d8c4069b439789e36eb9f"} {"question" "q-35292" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065011/state.json" "file_sha256" "f71acf576412692875148eeaadb4b7b5e216ba24093d8c4069b439789e36eb9f"} {"question" "q-35292" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065011/state.json" "file_sha256" "f71acf576412692875148eeaadb4b7b5e216ba24093d8c4069b439789e36eb9f"} {"question" "q-34854" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]})=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065011/state.json" "file_sha256" "f71acf576412692875148eeaadb4b7b5e216ba24093d8c4069b439789e36eb9f"} {"question" "q-34854" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ -6 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $1$ pivot column (or alternatively, it has exactly $1$ non-zero row). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]})=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065011/state.json" "file_sha256" "f71acf576412692875148eeaadb4b7b5e216ba24093d8c4069b439789e36eb9f"} {"question" "q-34854" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065011/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ad3f809a708b8c734e70cd58d6c8fe9d69e72465e11bf4865226f3aec4350e51"} {"question" "q-34854" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065011/state.json" "file_sha256" "f71acf576412692875148eeaadb4b7b5e216ba24093d8c4069b439789e36eb9f"} {"question" "q-34854" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "10"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065011/state.json" "file_sha256" "f71acf576412692875148eeaadb4b7b5e216ba24093d8c4069b439789e36eb9f"} {"question" "q-34854" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065011/state.json" "file_sha256" "f71acf576412692875148eeaadb4b7b5e216ba24093d8c4069b439789e36eb9f"} {"question" "e-5410" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the dimension of\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065011/assets/c0a1d1860f5be98c6606f191fd26b0f799bc182b7b4e7fdca542d693290f11e4.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065011/example-5410.json" "file_sha256" "3df133f600750b42b90b72e6032c3c2d041102bef7a4f1b3743c454aed42bcdb"} {"question" "e-5410" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form. We see that the matrix above has two pivot columns: the $1$st and the $2$nd columns. This tells us that $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2 \\}$ forms a basis for our span.\n\nSince $\\mathcal{B}$ consists of two vectors, any basis of our span will also have *exactly* two vectors. Therefore, the dimension of the span is $2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065011/assets/268ea5cffffb0765f5c4bbacff7f3253ad4243e0e1ffa77d3a8a5a7f794d62ba.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065011/example-5410.json" "file_sha256" "3df133f600750b42b90b72e6032c3c2d041102bef7a4f1b3743c454aed42bcdb"} {"question" "e-5411" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the dimension of\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065011/assets/668c8e17567f5635f3d39c98dced5c77c60a287cac8a92d29db2e8afefe94cec.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065011/example-5411.json" "file_sha256" "cd8e7ee4fcc24b66fd56560b8742a45231b4cb99f40bbfe0bcf4c1ab1508ab29"} {"question" "e-5411" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -6 & 3 & -15 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the matrix above has one pivot columns: the $1$st column. This tells us that $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1 \\}$ forms a basis for our span.\n\nSince $\\mathcal{B}$ consists of one vector, any basis of our span will also have *exactly* one vector. Therefore, the dimension of the span is $1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065011/assets/4196e42d01f879b24974be9c0d1bc456c4894cb19b832efab46f3cdb8a28b644.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065011/example-5411.json" "file_sha256" "cd8e7ee4fcc24b66fd56560b8742a45231b4cb99f40bbfe0bcf4c1ab1508ab29"}] "usage" {"e-5410" [] "e-5411" [] "q-34854" []} "basis" 2148 "retractions" [[17592186120297 :question/problem "What is $dim(Span{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}})$?"] [17592186119586 :question/problem "Determine the dimension of\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186119586 :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form. We see that the matrix above has two pivot columns: the $1$st and the $2$nd columns. This tells us that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2 \\}\n$$\n\nforms a basis for our span.\n\nSince $\\mathcal{B}$ consists of two vectors, any basis of our span will also have *exactly* two vectors. Therefore, the dimension of the span is $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186119587 :question/problem "Determine the dimension of\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186119587 :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -6 & 3 & -15 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} + 3R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} + (-2)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the matrix above has one pivot columns: the $1$st column. This tells us that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{v}_1 \\}\n$$\n\nforms a basis for our span.\n\nSince $\\mathcal{B}$ consists of one vector, any basis of our span will also have *exactly* one vector. Therefore, the dimension of the span is $1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186118258 17592186118259 17592186118260 17592186118261 17592186118262] "immutable_fields" [[17592186118257 :answer-field/correct] [17592186118257 :answer-field/id] [17592186118257 :answer-field/key] [17592186118257 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
